.TH "build-autoinstall-iso" "8" "2026-09-11" "ltsp6"
.SH "NAME"
build\-autoinstall\-iso \(em Build an unattended Debian installer ISO from a preseed
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBbuild\-autoinstall\-iso\fR \fInetinst.iso\fR \fIpreseed.cfg\fR \fIname\fR [\fIoutdir\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
Takes a plain Debian netinst image and a preseed file and produces an ISO that
installs without any interaction and powers the machine off when it is done.
Every guest in the lab \(em the domain controller, the load balancers, the image
server and the application servers \(em is installed this way, because clicking
through debian\-installer once per rebuild does not scale and should not depend
on anyone remembering which answers they gave last time.
.PP
\fIname\fR becomes both the volume id (\fBdebian\-auto\-\fR\fIname\fR) and the
output file name (\fIname\fR\fB\-auto.iso\fR). \fIoutdir\fR defaults to the
directory holding the source ISO, which on a Proxmox node is usually
/var/lib/vz/template/iso.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
.IP "\fInetinst.iso\fP" 4
The source image. Read only; it is loop mounted, never modified.
.IP "\fIpreseed.cfg\fP" 4
The preseed. The ones under server/tools/preseed encode the machine's role in
their package list \(em that is where role separation actually lives.
.IP "\fIname\fP" 4
Volume id and output file name.
.IP "\fIoutdir\fP" 4
Where to write the result. Defaults to the source ISO's directory.
.SH "EXAMPLE"
.PP
The hostname is per instance, so build one medium per guest and change only
that line:
.PP
.nf
for host in addc1 ltsp\-root ltsp\-app1 ltsp\-app2; do
    sed "s|^d\-i netcfg/get_hostname string .*|d\-i netcfg/get_hostname string $host|" \
        preseed/ltsp\-app.cfg > "/root/$host.cfg"
    build\-autoinstall\-iso /var/lib/vz/template/iso/debian\-13.6.0\-amd64\-netinst.iso \
        "/root/$host.cfg" "$host"
done
.fi
.SH "NOTES"
.PP
Two things this script had to learn the hard way, and both are worth knowing
before modifying it.
.PP
It replaces \fBisolinux/isolinux.cfg\fR outright rather than adding an include.
A \fBdefault\fR directive inside an included fragment does not win over the one
in the main file, and the first version of this script produced media that
booted the speech\-synthesis installer instead of the preseeded install \(em with
no error, because that is a perfectly valid boot entry.
.PP
It takes the isohybrid MBR from the source ISO, so the result boots on BIOS
firmware as well as UEFI. Without it, only UEFI machines start.
.PP
The installer stalls at \(lqName server addresses\(rq if the DHCP server on the
segment advertises no resolver. dnsmasq configured with \fBport=0\fR does exactly
that; hand out \fBoption:dns\-server\fR explicitly. See setup\-ltsp\-root(8).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBsetup\-ltsp\-root\fP(8),
\fBprovision\-samba\-ad\-dc\fP(8),
\fBsetup\-ltsp\-app\fP(8),
\fBxorriso\fP(1),
/usr/share/doc/ltsp\-server/HOWTO\-lab\-from\-scratch.
.SH "AVAILABILITY"
.PP
\fBbuild\-autoinstall\-iso\fR is part of the \fBltsp\-server\fP package; the
latest version is available from \fIhttps://github.com/MAButz/ltsp6\fR.
